# etude-aul.4 plan

ANCESTOR: etude-aul (P3) — empirically compare phase-gate prompt variants against recorded artifacts and ground-truth labels; the measured prototype result was Variant B at 2/2 versus Variant A at 1/2, and the three closed siblings now provide the corpus, evaluator, and scoring machinery this CLI exposes.

BLAST RADIUS: none: worktree and repo only. Gate scratch and built-binary manual-test fixtures use one bounded temporary directory each and are removed; no host packages, services, containers, credentials, or shared state are changed.

TRUST BOUNDARY: prompt variant files, labels JSON, cohort selector, and recorded run artifacts are user-controlled inputs and therefore untrusted. Existing parsers, identifier validation, refstore reads, and ExecJudge isolation are the enforcement points. The configured judge command is operator-trusted executable code; arbitrary same-process code execution is outside scope.

TOPOLOGY: single foreground CLI process. It evaluates cells sequentially; there are no new goroutines, shared writers, or concurrent mutation paths.

DURABILITY CLASS: at-most-once per process invocation for each variant/fixture cell. Gate-bench evaluation is read-only with respect to run refs and does not persist eval results; a judge may have its own external effects under the existing trusted judge-command contract.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete user-visible surface is (1) the unchanged replay-vs-original mode, (2) gate-mode argument and flag validation, (3) built-in run-refs cohort resolution, (4) prompt-file loading and one evaluation for every variant/fixture cell, (5) optional explicit labels with progression-proxy fallback and loud circularity warnings, (6) per-variant confusion-matrix rates, (7) per-cell verdict rendering including unlabeled cells, and (8) per-cell judge failure reporting with non-zero exit when no cell succeeds. This is a single-site CLI orchestration change; no other command registration or storage writer is changed.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: none

## Decision: extend the existing command

Extend the current `etude bench` Cobra leaf with a mutually exclusive gate-benchmark mode rather than add `etude bench gate`.

The decision is grounded in `internal/cli/bench.go` as landed:

- `bench` is one leaf command with the shipped positional form `bench <stage>`, not an existing command group.
- It already owns judge resolution, judge-model isolation, timeouts, the `--last` cohort limit, dependency injection for tests, and human-readable aggregate/per-cell reporting.
- The required public syntax is `bench --gate <phase> ...`. Converting the leaf into a command group would either break `bench <stage>` or require a default-subcommand compatibility shim, adding ambiguity and regression risk without adding useful separation.
- Gate mode does fundamentally different work (evaluate recorded artifacts directly rather than replay them), so the implementation will make the modes explicit and mutually exclusive: conditional argument validation, gate-only/replay-only flag rejection, and separate `runReplayBench`/`runGateBench` orchestration paths behind the same command.

`--cohort run-refs` selects the built-in `bench.RunRefsSource`; `--last N` remains the shared size selector. The injected `bench.CorpusSource` seam remains available to tests and future adapters. Unknown cohort selectors fail clearly; this bead does not invent or import a beads/Dolt adapter.

Each `--variant` value is a prompt file path and its exact flag value is the stable variant name in output. Gate mode requires at least two distinct variants, `--cohort`, and `--judge` (or existing judge config). `--labels` is optional: explicit labels take precedence, and missing explicit labels fall back to the progression proxy with the existing circularity warning surfaced in output.

## Implementation

Change only the allowed files:

- `internal/cli/bench.go`
  - add gate-mode flags and conditional Cobra argument validation while preserving `bench <stage>` behavior;
  - add an injectable `bench.CorpusSource` field and production resolver for `run-refs`;
  - load and validate distinct prompt files and optional strict labels JSON;
  - evaluate every variant/fixture cell through `eval.GateEvaluator` using exactly one `gate-prompt` context input;
  - convert `Score.Passed` through `bench.GateVerdictFromPassed`, resolve labels, aggregate with `bench.ScoreGatePredictions`, and render variant scores/confusion matrices, every successful cell, warnings, and failures;
  - continue after an individual cell failure and exit non-zero only if no cell succeeds, matching existing bench partial-failure semantics.
- `internal/cli/bench_test.go`
  - extend the existing injected-command harness with a fake corpus source and content-aware gate judge;
  - preserve all existing replay-mode tests unchanged in meaning.
- `docs/bench.md`
  - document both modes, exact syntax, selector/variant/labels contracts, proxy warning and final-plan limitation, output orientation, partial failures, and manual examples.

## Planned regressions

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateHappyPathReportsVariantScoresAndCells` — two variants over labeled fixtures produce distinct confusion matrices and every cell row.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateUsesProgressionProxyAndWarns` — absent labels use the proxy and print the exact circularity warning.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateExplicitLabelsOverrideProxy` — explicit labels win for a matching key.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGatePartialJudgeFailureContinues` — one failed cell is reported while successful cells still score and exit zero.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateAllJudgeFailuresExitNonZero` — zero successful cells returns a clear error.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateValidation` — rejects positional args in gate mode, fewer than two/distinct variants, missing or unknown cohort, unreadable variant/labels files, invalid labels, and replay-only flags.

Planned regression: `TestBenchReplayModeRejectsGateOnlyFlags` — legacy mode fails clearly instead of silently ignoring gate flags.

Planned regression: `TestBenchHelpDocumentsGateMode` — help shows the required `--gate`, repeated `--variant`, `--cohort`, and optional `--labels` surface while retaining legacy flags.

Planned regression: existing `TestBenchHappyPath` and the complete current bench test suite remain green, proving `bench <stage>` compatibility.

